\subsection{MLP Adapter Control}
\label{app:mlp_adapter}

To verify that re-encoding effectiveness is not specific to cross-attention, we train a parameter-matched MLP adapter as a control. The adapter is a 3-layer feed-forward network with expansion ratio 4 (hidden dimension $4 \times d_{\text{model}}$), using average pooling for token reduction from 128 to $M$ tokens. Total parameters: 22.14M, matched to the cross-attention module's 22.2M. Training follows the identical protocol as Smart Compress: SmolVLA-Base checkpoint, 100K steps, learning rate $10^{-4}$, batch size 4, same LIBERO dataset. At $M = 32$ (75\% reduction), the MLP adapter achieves $63.4 \pm 2.6$\% SR over 3 seeds, statistically indistinguishable from the cross-attention result ($61.2 \pm 1.4$\%; two-sample $t$-test, $p = 0.26$). This confirms that the re-encoding benefit does not depend on the specific module architecture.


